### What is the Maximize Capital problem?

The problem gives an initial capital W, a list of projects each with a capital requirement and a profit, and a limit K on the number of projects. Completing a project adds its profit to the capital. The task is to choose at most K projects to maximise the final capital.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class MaximizeCapital {
    public int findMaximizedCapital(int k, int w, int[] profits, int[] capital) {
        int n = profits.length;
        // min-heap by capital requirement
        PriorityQueue<int[]> minCap = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));
        // max-heap by profit
        PriorityQueue<int[]> maxProfit = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b[1] - a[1]);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) minCap.offer(new int[]{capital[i], profits[i]});

        for (int round = 0; round < k; round++) {
            while (!minCap.isEmpty() && minCap.peek()[0] <= w) {
                maxProfit.offer(minCap.poll());
            }
            if (maxProfit.isEmpty()) break; // no affordable project
            w += maxProfit.poll()[1];
        }
        return w;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] profits = {1, 2, 3};
        int[] capital = {0, 1, 1};
        System.out.println("Max capital: "
            + new MaximizeCapital().findMaximizedCapital(2, 0, profits, capital));
    }
}
```

```text
Max capital: 4
```

### Why does greedy give the optimal answer?

Capital only grows because profits are non-negative. Once a project becomes affordable, it stays affordable in all future rounds. Therefore, deferring a project never makes it unavailable. Taking the maximum profit each round grows the capital as fast as possible, which unlocks the most expensive projects soonest. This is the classic "stays ahead" argument for greedy correctness.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class GreedyCorrectnessProof {
    // 1. Capital only grows (profits >= 0)
    // 2. Once affordable, always affordable
    // 3. Taking max profit grows capital fastest
    // 4. Faster growth unlocks more projects sooner
    // 5. Therefore greedy is optimal

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Greedy is correct because capital only grows.");
        System.out.println("Taking max profit each round cannot be beaten.");
    }
}
```

### How do the two heaps cooperate?

The min-heap gates by affordability: it surfaces the cheapest projects first. The max-heap ranks by profitability: it gives the best return among affordable projects. Each round, the unlock step moves all newly affordable projects from the min-heap to the max-heap. Then the pick step takes the most profitable one. The min-heap empties over time as capital grows and more projects become affordable.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class TwoHeapTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] profits = {1, 2, 3};
        int[] capital = {0, 1, 1};
        int w = 0;

        PriorityQueue<int[]> minCap = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));
        PriorityQueue<int[]> maxProfit = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b[1] - a[1]);
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) minCap.offer(new int[]{capital[i], profits[i]});

        // Round 1: w=0, unlock projects with cap <= 0
        while (!minCap.isEmpty() && minCap.peek()[0] <= w) maxProfit.offer(minCap.poll());
        System.out.println("Round 1 affordable: " + maxProfit.size());
        w += maxProfit.poll()[1];
        System.out.println("After round 1: w=" + w);

        // Round 2: w=1, unlock projects with cap <= 1
        while (!minCap.isEmpty() && minCap.peek()[0] <= w) maxProfit.offer(minCap.poll());
        System.out.println("Round 2 affordable: " + maxProfit.size());
        w += maxProfit.poll()[1];
        System.out.println("After round 2: w=" + w);
    }
}
```

```text
Round 1 affordable: 1
After round 1: w=1
Round 2 affordable: 2
After round 2: w=4
```

### How do you handle money with BigDecimal?

In a real banking application, capital and profits are money and should use BigDecimal. The comparators use compareTo instead of subtraction, and the capital accumulation uses BigDecimal.add.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class MaximizeCapitalBigDecimal {
    record Deal(BigDecimal required, BigDecimal profit) {}

    public BigDecimal maximize(int k, BigDecimal w, List<Deal> deals) {
        PriorityQueue<Deal> minCap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparing(Deal::required));
        PriorityQueue<Deal> maxProfit = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparing(Deal::profit).reversed());
        minCap.addAll(deals);

        for (int round = 0; round < k; round++) {
            while (!minCap.isEmpty()
                    && minCap.peek().required().compareTo(w) <= 0) {
                maxProfit.offer(minCap.poll());
            }
            if (maxProfit.isEmpty()) break;
            w = w.add(maxProfit.poll().profit());
        }
        return w;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Deal> deals = List.of(
            new Deal(new BigDecimal("0"), new BigDecimal("10000")),
            new Deal(new BigDecimal("5000"), new BigDecimal("25000")),
            new Deal(new BigDecimal("10000"), new BigDecimal("50000"))
        );
        BigDecimal result = new MaximizeCapitalBigDecimal()
            .maximize(2, BigDecimal.ZERO, deals);
        System.out.println("Final capital: " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Final capital: 35000
```

### How is this different from a knapsack problem?

| Feature | Maximize Capital | 0/1 Knapsack |
|---|---|---|
| Selection | sequential with growing budget | all items available at once |
| Budget grows | yes, profits add to capital | no, weight is consumed |
| Greedy works | yes (capital only grows) | no (greedy fails for knapsack) |
| Complexity | O(n log n + k log n) | O(n * W) pseudo-polynomial |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CapitalVsKnapsack {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Capital: budget grows, greedy works.");
        System.out.println("Knapsack: budget consumed, need DP.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include no affordable projects (return initial capital), K larger than the number of projects (complete all affordable ones), all projects requiring more capital than available, and projects with zero profit.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CapitalEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MaximizeCapital solver = new MaximizeCapital();

        // No affordable projects
        System.out.println("No affordable: "
            + solver.findMaximizedCapital(3, 0, new int[]{5}, new int[]{10}));

        // K > projects
        System.out.println("K > n: "
            + solver.findMaximizedCapital(10, 0, new int[]{1,2}, new int[]{0,0}));
    }
}
```

```text
No affordable: 0
K > n: 3
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to stop early when the max-heap is empty, which would cause a NoSuchElementException. Another mistake is not draining all affordable projects from the min-heap each round, missing projects that became affordable due to increased capital.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: only moves one project per round instead of all affordable
if (!minCap.isEmpty() && minCap.peek()[0] <= w) maxProfit.offer(minCap.poll());
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: drains all affordable projects
while (!minCap.isEmpty() && minCap.peek()[0] <= w) maxProfit.offer(minCap.poll());
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows the time complexity is O(n log n) for building the min-heap plus O(k log n) for the K rounds of unlock-and-pick. They also know the greedy correctness relies on capital only growing (non-negative profits). If profits could be negative, greedy would not work and the problem would become a variant of knapsack with DP. In production at National Bank of India, the investment desk uses this logic with BigDecimal and real deal data from the database.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorCapitalNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Time: O(n log n + k log n).");
        System.out.println("Greedy works because capital only grows.");
        System.out.println("If profits can be negative, need DP instead.");
        System.out.println("Use BigDecimal for real money.");
    }
}
```
